Syntax: jam <weapon> <direction>

Jamming is a looter's answer to being followed.  By working a slim piercing
weapon into the keyhole of a closed door and snapping it off flush, a thief can
bind the door shut behind them.  Any blade light enough to backstab with will
serve; heavier weapons will not seat properly in the gap.

The door must be closed and unlocked before it can be jammed.  A door that is
already locked offers nowhere for the blade to bite.

A successful jam locks the door from both sides.  How stubborn the resulting
lock is depends on the thief's level and their mastery of the skill.  The
weapon is destroyed in the process and cannot be recovered, so a prudent
looter carries something cheap for the purpose.  A failed attempt leaves the
blade intact and may be tried again.

A weapon bearing a monogram is no use here.  Leaving your own name wedged in
a keyhole rather defeats the purpose.

Nothing about a jam defeats a key.  Anyone carrying the door's key can unlock
it as though the jam were an ordinary lock.

See Also: PICKLOCK, DOORBASH
